#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa54bca0-f61d-569d-ac43-3edfc933e221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332581} {:answer/id #uuid "78d10572-47e8-5f06-9377-4158762acd1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332582} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ac4cf4-18c9-5e56-afe3-a201adc55a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332583} {:answer/id #uuid "72671062-989c-5748-ae57-796cb7b8a96e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332584} {:answer/id #uuid "d988ef17-7cd0-57cf-bedd-df476651cd7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d10572-47e8-5f06-9377-4158762acd1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332582} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b2aa5b5-7356-59c9-b5dc-04c7139ccd54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3cfc1fb-f2ea-5d4f-9851-aafa6955258e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141101" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{5}$\n- $y={x}^{10}$\n- $y=5{x}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{5},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{5})}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & =5{x}^{4}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{5}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{10},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=10{x}^{9}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{10})}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}5{x}^{9} \\\\ 5{x}^{9} & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{10}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=5{x}^{4},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{3}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5(5{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}25{x}^{3} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 5{x}^{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=5{x}^{4}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c618f7f9-2e7f-576b-96b7-ccc4a778ab6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343184} {:answer/id #uuid "d5006f89-2076-5f9e-9195-b0b4dcaf3d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343185} {:answer/id #uuid "8a396a12-b776-51b0-ada0-c0b218c79ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343186} {:answer/id #uuid "46ee5306-287b-577e-a689-c75e8d3a3aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343187} {:answer/id #uuid "62985bd6-aec5-560e-ac0b-0554a552069c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a396a12-b776-51b0-ada0-c0b218c79ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343186} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab99ede8-c73a-5acb-986d-481afaddbefa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e0065e9-3a69-5f85-ad17-2869bffc442f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141113" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,y(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{{2}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{4} \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f7c9cb-03bb-5f4a-bc2c-dac592eecf5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343191} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d131b7-f132-532b-9637-35c5693ffaad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343192} {:answer/id #uuid "57167bc3-7525-5a98-8e9c-f60d49407684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343193} {:answer/id #uuid "aa80debc-bf73-5dee-b7f3-16746495d52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343194} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff10567-e99b-581f-9a5c-3d8134aec462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57167bc3-7525-5a98-8e9c-f60d49407684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343193} :answer-field/id #uuid "e09e87c1-7fa9-5e7e-867d-2744524684a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8e92955-2aba-5ce5-9cc9-92df3e501cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-141114" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x},\\,y(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=Ax$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=Ax$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-1)=3$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =Ax \\\\ 3 & =A(-1) \\\\ 3 & =-A \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e46a7ae1-4011-5704-af42-e13cf11c2581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46a7ae1-4011-5704-af42-e13cf11c2581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343198} :answer-field/id #uuid "98285775-a9fb-56b7-be3d-393fa624d3e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "234ff426-43d9-539a-9bed-18f994374c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-352654" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y,\\,y(2)=5.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=5.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=5$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x} \\\\ 5 & =A{e}^{3\\cdot 2} \\\\ 5 & =A{e}^{6} \\\\ \\frac{5}{{e}^{6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278ca932-6bd2-5409-a12c-59e55f3a91f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304299} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb93bf5-9543-57f8-bc26-332e5dccb902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304300} {:answer/id #uuid "e93a9665-2338-5665-a5fb-0695e3b625bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304301} {:answer/id #uuid "f246ebc7-45e4-5632-8c1c-03c9ed1a380a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304302} {:answer/id #uuid "85570b73-32ed-53d3-9cac-646d81e759b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85570b73-32ed-53d3-9cac-646d81e759b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304303} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d3c876-9a73-5d7f-be91-1b4288459661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df42d33c-ab0b-52f4-82a5-1108ad30f682" :question/math-academy-id "q-79330" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={e}^{-4x}$\n- $y={e}^{4x}$\n- $y=4{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{-4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & \\overset{?}{=}4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 8{e}^{-4x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{4x} & =4{e}^{4x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={e}^{4x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=4{e}^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4(4{e}^{x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}16{e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 12{e}^{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=4{e}^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
